  :adr/body "# ADR 2799007123: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 7123 Plasterers job-site scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 7123\n-- Plasterers** only, Wave3 (production/trades) of the rollout, batch2\n(batch1 -- 7111/7112/7113/7114/7119/7121 -- already landed).\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"7123\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"7123\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Plasterers\" -- confirming the\npremise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's `:maturity` was\n`:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics :identity\n:audit-ledger]`, `:optional-technologies []` and `:operating-states\n[:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit]`, and no\n`:repo`/`:business-id`; no repository existed at\n`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7123` (`gh api` 404 confirmed before\nscaffolding). This ADR and its companion registry edit promote the\n`7123` entry to `:implemented`.\n\nPlasterers apply wall/ceiling finishing materials on active job sites\n-- real physical-safety stakes (dust exposure, scaffold hazards,\nmaterial handling). The controlling constraint for this vertical is\nthat the closed op allowlist must NEVER include any op that directly\nfinalizes a plastering-execution decision, or overrides a site safety\nofficer's judgment -- those decisions must always be a hard permanent\nblock or an always-escalate op, never auto-commit-eligible. This actor\nis deliberately scoped to JOB-SITE SCHEDULING/LOGISTICS COORDINATION\nONLY -- it never performs plastering work itself.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored closely for module SHAPE, the\nclosed-allowlist + independently-verified-provenance safety pattern,\nand the doc set is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7111)\n(House Builders: same wave, same job-site-scheduling/logistics-\ncoordination domain shape -- `store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`actor`\nsplit, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph, full doc set: README,\nCODE_OF_CONDUCT.md, CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, LICENSE\n(AGPL-3.0-or-later), SECURITY.md, docs/business-model.md,\ndocs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn), cloned and read in full this\nsession.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7123` as an OSS job-site\nscheduling/logistics-coordination actor for plasterers, following the\nSAME governed-actor architecture as every prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*`\nactor in this fleet: **Plasterer Advisor ⊣ PlastererGovernor** on a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph (`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`,\ngit-sha-pinned identically to the reference's `deps.edn` -- a\ndifferent library coordinate from the `langgraph-clj` used by the\nunrelated `20-actors/*` actor family), with an independent Governor,\nhuman-in-the-loop approval via `interrupt-before`, and an append-only\naudit ledger. All source is portable `.cljc` (no JVM interop); tests\nare `clojure.test` `.clj`, matching the reference repo's own layout.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **job-site scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never direct plastering-execution authority** -- it must NEVER\nbe designed to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising a\nplastering-execution decision (e.g. a specific wall or ceiling\nfinishing step), or any override of a site safety officer's judgment.\nThese are not merely gated behind escalation -- they are structurally\nabsent from the op-allowlist entirely.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- task/materials-usage/progress data logging\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a dust-exposure/scaffold-hazard/\n  site-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates, NEVER auto-commit eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- plastering-materials procurement\n  proposal\n\nThis actor has **NO op, anywhere in its allowlist, that resembles** a\nplastering-execution decision or a site-safety-officer-judgment\noverride. These are **structurally absent from the op-allowlist\nentirely**, not merely gated behind escalation -- an op such as\n`:finalize-plastering-decision` cannot even be proposed by the\nreference mock advisor (its `infer`/`rationale-for` functions only\never emit one of the four allowlist ops), and if any code path (a\nforged proposal, a future LLM advisor, a misbehaving fork) ever\nproduced such an op, the governor's closed-allowlist HARD check\n(`:unknown-op`) rejects it unconditionally and permanently. A second,\nindependent HARD check (`:scope-excluded-action`) names four\nconcretely-forbidden ops (`plasterer.governor/scope-excluded-ops`:\n`:finalize-plastering-decision`, `:authorize-plaster-application`,\n`:proceed-with-plastering-work`, `:override-safety-officer-judgment`)\nas defense-in-depth, and is verified by dedicated unit tests\n(`plasterer.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-plastering-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-plaster-application`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-plastering-work`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-safety-officer-judgment`).\n\n### Four HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Plasterer unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered plasterer record (`:no-plasterer`). Never\n   trusts the proposal's own claim of who the plasterer is.\n2. **Site unregistered** -- any proposal must cite an independently\n   registered job site (`:no-site`). Together with check 1, this\n   directly implements the CRITICAL invariant that a worker/site\n   record must be independently verified/registered before any\n   action.\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (a claim to directly actuate outside governance)\n   (`:no-actuation`).\n4. **Op not allowed / scope-excluded action** -- any `:op` outside the\n   closed four-op allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`),\n   and independently, any proposal whose `:op` names one of the four\n   concretely-forbidden ops above, OR whose free-text `:rationale`\n   names a finalization/execution action for proceeding with\n   plastering work, or overriding a site safety officer's judgment, is\n   a second HARD, PERMANENT block (`:scope-excluded-action`), evaluated\n   unconditionally on content -- defense-in-depth on top of the\n   allowlist check: even if a future change somehow widened the\n   allowlist, content-based rejection still independently blocks\n   plastering-execution/safety-override language wherever it appears\n   in free text.\n\n### One always-escalate (SOFT) gate, one threshold gate, plus the general confidence floor\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's implicit always-risky check). This is the\n  ONLY channel by which the robot may surface a dust-exposure/\n  scaffold-hazard/site-condition observation -- it never resolves the\n  concern itself, and the robot's role ends at \"here is the hazard\n  data,\" never \"here is what I recommend doing about site safety.\"\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000, inclusive boundary verified by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`) -- always needs human\n  sign-off, independent of confidence. This is an ESCALATION, not a\n  hard block, per the task's explicit domain design: an over-threshold\n  plastering-materials order is not itself unsafe, unlike a\n  plastering-execution or safety-officer-override attempt, which is\n  why it is handled by a different mechanism (soft escalate vs.\n  structural absence).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, carried over from the sibling ISIC/ISCO tracks)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare noun (e.g.\n\"plaster\", \"plastering\", \"safety\") can accidentally match inside the\nmock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- this is exactly the risk\nthis vertical's own vocabulary (a job-site coordination actor working\nadjacent to plastering work necessarily discusses those concepts to do\nits scheduling/logging job) could trigger if phrased carelessly.\n`plasterer.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased exclusively as\nfinalization/execution ACTIONS (e.g. \"proceed with the plastering\nwork\", \"authorize the plaster application\", \"override the site safety\nofficer's judgment\" -- matching the task's own worked example\nphrasing), never bare nouns, and\n`plasterer.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-on-scope-exclusion`\nis a dedicated regression test asserting every op in the closed\nallowlist, driven through the default mock advisor, never trips\n`:scope-excluded-action`. The mock advisor's own default rationale\ntemplate was deliberately written to contain the bare nouns (\"logged\nwork record for plasterer ...\", \"scheduled crew operation for\nplastering task ...\", \"... routed for site safety officer review\")\nWITHOUT containing any of the full finalization-action phrases, so the\nregression test is a genuine stress test of the bare-noun-vs-action-\nphrase distinction, not a vacuous pass. Green on first run in this\nsession -- the scope-exclusion self-trip guard itself was never\ntripped during development. `plasterer.actor-test/holds-a-scope-\nexcluded-op-even-at-high-confidence` additionally exercises the same\ninvariant through the full graph run, confirming a scope-excluded\nproposal never reaches `commit-record!` regardless of disposition\npath.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"7123\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-7123` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-7123`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live from the registry via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary`, not assumed, immediately\n  before the edit, to account for concurrent sibling landings in this\n  same batch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 21 tests, 45 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's\n  `:test/counts` and in the task report, independently re-verified\n  against a brand-new fresh clone of the pushed repository (not just\n  the local working copy).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely via\n  the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent with\n  sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`, \"Remove all .md, normalize ADRs to\n  tx-data\") -- a single-entity EDN vector with an `:adr/body` string\n  blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n- Full doc set published: README.md, CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md,\n  CONTRIBUTING.md, GOVERNANCE.md, SECURITY.md, LICENSE\n  (AGPL-3.0-or-later, byte-identical to the `cloud-itonami-isco-7111`\n  reference's LICENSE text), docs/business-model.md,\n  docs/operator-guide.md, blueprint.edn.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-plastering-decision` that is\n  merely gated behind an always-escalate list (the shape\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-3313` uses for `:approve-over-ceiling-posting`):\n  rejected outright per the task's explicit domain-design guidance --\n  an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to finalize a\n  plastering-execution decision exists in the code path and is merely\n  deferred to a human gate. For a decision touching physical worker\n  safety (dust exposure, scaffold hazards, material handling), the\n  capability itself must not exist. The only op related to site-safety\n  escalation is `:flag-safety-concern`, which surfaces the need for\n  human/site-safety-officer attention and never itself decides,\n  authorizes, or defers a plastering-execution decision for later\n  automatic execution.\n- Making the supply-cost ceiling a HARD block (mirroring the 3313\n  reference's transaction-amount-ceiling HARD check): rejected per the\n  task's explicit domain design -- an over-threshold plastering-\n  materials order is not itself unsafe, so it is handled as a soft\n  escalate gate, not a hard block; only the plastering-execution/\n  safety-officer-override class is a hard, unconditional block.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"plaster\"\n  \"plastering\" \"safety\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known\n  self-trip bug class described above (and CLAUDE.md's own worked\n  example for this exact task), and per the explicit instruction to\n  phrase scope-exclusion terms as finalization/execution ACTIONS,\n  never bare nouns -- this actor's own domain necessarily discusses\n  plaster/plastering/safety-officer concepts to do its\n  scheduling/logging job, so a bare-noun list would risk self-blocking\n  the actor's own legitimate proposals (regression-tested directly\n  against rationale text that deliberately contains those bare nouns).\n- Merging `plasterer` and `site` into a single entity (only checking\n  one provenance record): rejected -- the two-entity provenance shape\n  (independently-registered plasterer AND independently-registered\n  site) matches the CRITICAL invariant that a \"worker/site record must\n  be independently verified/registered before any action\" and the\n  established pattern from prior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actors with\n  two-sided provenance checks.\n\n## Itemized safety confirmation\n\nEvery op in this actor's closed proposal-op allowlist, and why it is\nsafe under the constraints above:\n\n1. **`:log-work-record`** -- task/materials-usage/progress data\n   logging only. Requires an independently registered plasterer AND\n   site (`:no-plasterer`/`:no-site`). Not a plastering-execution or\n   safety-officer decision in any respect -- it is a\n   records-management action.\n2. **`:schedule-crew-operation`** -- crew/task scheduling proposal\n   only. Cannot carry a plastering-execution decision (structurally\n   absent from the op's field shape; any such attempt via free text is\n   caught by the `:scope-excluded-action` content check). Does not\n   authorize, finalize, or draw any conclusion about a specific\n   wall/ceiling finishing step -- it is a calendar/logistics action.\n3. **`:flag-safety-concern`** -- surfaces a dust-exposure/scaffold-\n   hazard/site-condition concern to a human/site safety officer;\n   ALWAYS escalates, unconditionally, at every confidence level, NEVER\n   auto-commit eligible. This is the ONLY channel through which a\n   safety observation may be surfaced, and it explicitly never\n   resolves, decides, or acts on anything itself -- the site safety\n   officer reviews and acts entirely on their own authority.\n4. **`:coordinate-supply-order`** -- plastering-materials procurement\n   proposal only (item, cost). Not a plastering-execution or\n   authorization decision of any kind -- it is a purchasing-logistics\n   action, gated by ordinary cost-ceiling escalation (above 2000\n   always escalates).\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase, resembles\na plastering-execution decision (e.g. proceeding with a specific\nwall/ceiling finishing step) or a site-safety-officer-judgment\noverride -- these categories are structurally absent, not gated,\nconfirmed by: (a) the closed four-op allowlist itself\n(`plasterer.governor/allowed-ops`), (b) four concretely-named\nforbidden-op fixtures rejected at the governor-unit level\n(`plasterer.governor/scope-excluded-ops`,\n`plasterer.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-op-finalize-plastering-decision`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-authorize-plaster-application`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-proceed-with-plastering-work`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-op-override-safety-officer-judgment`), and\n(c) a content-based scope-exclusion check independent of the `:op`\nfield (`plasterer.governor/scope-excluded-phrases`,\n`.../contains-excluded-phrase?`), tested directly against a rationale\nthat attempts to proceed with plastering work or override a site\nsafety officer's judgment even via an otherwise-allowlisted op\n(`plasterer.governor-test/hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-even-with-allowlisted-op`,\n`.../hard-on-scope-excluded-rationale-override-safety-officer`).\n"}]
